WHAT IS MEASURED AND WHAT IS NOT

Every performance figure published by this exercise is measured on one Apple
Silicon laptop, by the demonstration run in run_demo.py. That run builds a model
of 2,084,224 parameters (d_model 128) -- NOT the 5,774,080-parameter default in
model.py, which nothing here trains. Naming the wrong one has already happened in
this file, so state which model a figure came from whenever you quote it.

The corpus is 10,649,549 training tokens across 57 shards, which a full run of
10,485,760 token positions crosses 1.0139 times: read once, not memorised. A
further 1,093,019 tokens are written as held-out shards the firewall refuses to
serve. These are the figures in results/corpus_build.json, which is tracked, and
they are the only ones that should ever be quoted here.

That corpus is itself a correction, and the earlier number was in this paragraph
for weeks. It was once 2,185,575 tokens -- 4.8 crossings, and shaped to the
mixture, roughly 30 epochs of the web lane against 0.41 of an epoch of the
agentic one: the lane funded most heavily read thirty times over, the lane funded
least never read through once. Nothing failed. The shards read fine and the loss
curve looked normal, which is exactly why the corpus builder now refuses to build
below one epoch rather than leaving it to be noticed.

The design is ABOUT the mechanics of a run four orders of magnitude larger in
tokens. It was never run at that scale, and any figure describing that scale is
labelled as modelled rather than measured.

Efficiency figures quoted from the OPUS paper (4.7% overhead) and from
LightningLM (3.2%) are theirs. They are not reproduced here and are not claimed.

WHAT REPLAY PROVES, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT

Replay reproduces INPUTS exactly: batch ids, token spans, and the hashes of the
token block, loss mask, position ids and segment ids. It does NOT reproduce
losses, selector scores, gradient norms or checkpoint weight hashes -- floating
point reduction order differs across devices and thread counts -- and those are
reported against a stated tolerance rather than asserted equal.

NO EVALUATION CONTENT IS PUBLISHED

Evaluation and validation shards are registered so the system can refuse them,
never so they can be read. The firewall is deliberately two-sided: the shard
carries a never-train tag AND the trainer independently asks the registry before
consuming anything.

NO DATASET IS REDISTRIBUTED

This exercise fetches openly-licensed corpora, verifies each licence at fetch
time from the source itself, and refuses anything that declares none. It does not
host, mirror or redistribute any dataset. Links point at the publishers, and each
dataset's own licence governs its use.
